.TH MDSPEAK 1 "October 2026" "mdspeak 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mdspeak \- render Markdown in the terminal and read it aloud with a local TTS model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdspeak
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B mdspeak
.B \-t
.I TEXT
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B mdspeak
.B setup
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdspeak
takes Markdown, prints it formatted in the terminal (headings, lists, emphasis, code blocks), and speaks it aloud using
.BR piper (1),
a fast neural text-to-speech engine that runs entirely on the local machine.
No network access is used except by
.BR "mdspeak setup" .
.PP
Before speaking, the Markdown is converted to plain prose: markup characters are dropped, link text is kept, image alt text is introduced with "Image:", list items and table rows become separate sentences, and fenced or indented code blocks are replaced by the phrase "Code block omitted."
.PP
Input is taken from, in order of precedence: the
.B \-t
text, the
.I FILE
arguments (read in order, a file named
.B \-
means standard input), or standard input when no files are given. If no input is given and standard input is a terminal, the usage message is printed and the exit status is 2.
.PP
Like other filters,
.B mdspeak
prints nothing on success when its output is not a terminal. The formatted Markdown is shown only when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset and
.B TERM
is not "dumb"; with
.B \-\-no\-speak
and output that is not a terminal, the Markdown is copied through unchanged, like
.BR cat (1).
.PP
.B mdspeak
is the counterpart of
.BR mdhear (1),
which turns speech into Markdown.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text " \fITEXT\fR"
Speak
.I TEXT
(Markdown allowed) instead of reading files or standard input. The two characters
.B \e\|n
are converted to a real newline.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-voice " \fIVOICE\fR"
Voice to use: either the name of a model in the voices directory (for example
.BR en_US\-lessac\-medium )
or a path to a
.B .onnx
file. A matching
.B .onnx.json
file must sit beside the model. Default:
.BR en_US\-lessac\-medium .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the speech to a WAV file instead of playing it.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-speed " \fIN\fR"
Speaking speed multiplier. 1.0 is normal, 1.5 is faster, 0.8 is slower. Default: 1.0.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Speak only; do not print the rendered Markdown.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-speak
Print the rendered Markdown only; do not speak.
.TP
.BI \-\-completions " SHELL"
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.B elvish
or
.B powershell
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B setup
Download Piper and the default English voice into
.IR ~/.local/share/mdspeak .
This needs
.BR curl (1)
and
.BR tar (1)
and about 90 MB of disk space. Already-downloaded files are skipped, so it is safe to re-run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a README aloud:
.PP
.RS
.B mdspeak README.md
.RE
.PP
Read faster without printing anything:
.PP
.RS
.B mdspeak \-q \-s 1.4 notes.md
.RE
.PP
Pipe text in:
.PP
.RS
.B cat article.md | mdspeak \-
.RE
.PP
Save an audiobook-style recording:
.PP
.RS
.B mdspeak \-q chapter1.md \-o chapter1.wav
.RE
.PP
Preview how a document renders without speaking:
.PP
.RS
.B mdspeak \-n draft.md
.RE
.PP
Round trip with
.BR mdhear (1):
.PP
.RS
.B mdspeak \-q \-t "Hello there" \-o a.wav && mdhear a.wav
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/mdspeak/piper/piper
The Piper executable installed by
.BR "mdspeak setup" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/mdspeak/voices/
Voice models (\fI*.onnx\fR and \fI*.onnx.json\fR). Additional voices from the Piper voice collection can be dropped in here and selected with
.BR \-\-voice .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MDSPEAK_VOICE
Default for
.BR \-\-voice .
.TP
.B MDSPEAK_SPEED
Default for
.BR \-\-speed .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and non\-empty, the formatted rendering is not shown.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
If set, the data directory is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/mdspeak
instead of
.IR ~/.local/share/mdspeak .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Any error: missing input, Piper or voice not installed, playback failure, unreadable file. Messages are written to standard error as "mdspeak: message".
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage (unknown option, or no input given on a terminal).
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.BR aplay (1)
(alsa-utils) for playback, which also works through PipeWire and PulseAudio ALSA compatibility layers.
.SH BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
Linux only. English voice by default. Code blocks are skipped rather than read. There is no way to pause or seek; press Ctrl\-C to stop playback.
.PP
The Piper subcommand name
.B setup
cannot be used as a file name; write
.B ./setup
instead.
.PP
Report issues at https://github.com/ogden-marrow/mdspeak/issues.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdhear (1),
.BR piper (1),
.BR aplay (1)
